INT. FLOODED COUNTY ARCHIVE - BASEMENT - NIGHT

Knee-deep brown water. Floating file boxes. A string of emergency bulbs. ATTORNEY SIGRID HOLMGREN-DADA (40s, waders over her suit) wades between shelves. Her paralegal CASIMIR "CASS" LEBEAU (20s, headlamp) carries a dripping box. And the archivist, MR. WINSTON PELLEGRINO (70s, rubber apron), shouts from the staircase above.

                    WINSTON (O.S.)
          The 1974 deed books are on the third shelf!

                    SIGRID
          They're underwater, Winston!

                    WINSTON (O.S.)
          Then they're on the bottom shelf!

                    CASS
          Ms. Holmgren-Dada, found it. Box 41.

She lunges across the aisle. He lifts the box. She grabs the other side. They tug, and the box splits. Papers fan out across the black water.

                    SIGRID
          Cass!

                    CASS
          It was your end that tore!

                    WINSTON (O.S.)
          What's happening down there?

                    SIGRID
          Nothing!

                    CASS
          Everything!

They scramble after the pages, snatching them from the current. Sigrid pins one against a shelf, and Cass pins another against his chest. A third floats past, and both reach, and both miss.

                    WINSTON (O.S.)
          Is it the deed?

Sigrid fishes the page out of the water and reads it by headlamp. Her face falls, then hardens.

                    SIGRID
          (quietly)
          Winston, come down here. Bring a camera.

                    WINSTON (O.S.)
          I'm too old for stairs!

                    CASS
          It's the signature, isn't it.

                    SIGRID
          It's a forgery.

The three of them stand in the black water, one overhead bulb swinging above them, as the page drips in her hand.

                    WINSTON (O.S.)
          (pause)
          I'm coming.
